# PHASE 5 — FREQUENT CONVERSATIONS DIRECTORY

Completely rebuild and correct the Frequent module.

Frequent must function as a people and conversation directory using conversation metadata and aggregated collection information.

**Frequent is NOT a message viewer.**

## Information permitted in Frequent

Frequent may display:

- Display name or nickname, when available.
- Avatar, when available.
- Conversation identifier internally when necessary.
- Total collected message count for that conversation.
- Last interaction date, when reliably available.
- Other necessary non-content conversation metadata.

## Information prohibited in Frequent

Frequent must NEVER display:

- Actual message text.
- Message previews.
- Message snippets.
- Quotes from conversations.
- Attachments or message content.
- AI-generated message summaries.
- Message-content-based hover previews.
- Expandable message-content sections.

The Frequent module must not render actual conversation content in any form.

## Frequent functionality

- Automatically populate the directory using the existing shared collection and conversation metadata.
- Use the existing collection and storage systems.
- Do not create another collector.
- Do not create another database.
- Do not duplicate the underlying message data.
- Support searching.
- Support sorting.
- Support organization of conversation entries.
- Display aggregated message counts.
- Display available names and avatars.
- Handle missing metadata gracefully.
- Provide a clear empty state.
- Support proper loading and error states.
- Allow users to select a conversation entry.

## Conversation navigation

When a user clicks a conversation entry:

- Navigate to the corresponding Discord conversation using supported UI mechanisms.
- Use reliable conversation identifiers.
- Do not merely open a static analytics entry.
- Handle unavailable conversations gracefully.
- Do not claim navigation succeeded if it did not.

## Privacy and separation

Frequent must remain a metadata-based directory.

Use the shared data layer only to retrieve the information necessary to identify, organize and count conversations.

Never render message content within Frequent, including through secondary components, tooltips, previews or expandable sections.

Add tests that verify the separation between conversation metadata and message contents.

