# PHASE 8 — PAW-HC ACCESSIBILITY FRAMEWORK

Accessibility must be built into the shared UI architecture.

Do not treat accessibility as a superficial collection of toggles or an afterthought.

Design the interface to accommodate different interaction, sensory, cognitive, motor and visual needs.

## Cognitive accessibility

Where technically feasible:

- Reduce unnecessary clutter.
- Simplify navigation.
- Reduce memory burden.
- Maintain predictable layouts.
- Provide consistent controls.
- Make actions understandable.
- Provide clear feedback.
- Reduce unnecessary steps.
- Support simpler UI configurations.
- Avoid overwhelming information presentation.
- Make empty and error states understandable.
- Allow configurable visual density.

## Motor accessibility

Where technically feasible:

- Increase interaction target sizes.
- Reduce precision requirements.
- Support keyboard navigation.
- Maintain clear focus.
- Avoid unnecessary interaction complexity.
- Support accessible control spacing.
- Avoid interactions that require excessive repetitive movement.

## Visual accessibility

Implement:

- Accessible contrast.
- Scalable text.
- Clear typography.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Meaningful labels.
- Accessible icons.
- Color-independent information.
- Adjustable visual density where feasible.

## Sensory accessibility

Where technically feasible:

- Reduced motion.
- Adjustable visual stimulation.
- Reduced unnecessary animations.
- Configurable interface density.
- Clear visual hierarchy.
- Less distracting presentation.

## Screen reader accessibility

Implement:

- Semantic UI structures.
- Accessible labels.
- Appropriate roles.
- Meaningful status announcements.
- Accessible navigation.
- Logical reading order.
- Properly labeled controls.
- Accessible tables and graph alternatives.

## Keyboard accessibility

Ensure:

- Keyboard navigation throughout the interface.
- Logical focus order.
- Visible focus.
- Accessible interactive controls.
- Proper focus handling when opening and closing panels.
- Focus restoration where appropriate.
- No keyboard traps.

Accessibility settings themselves must be accessible.

---

# PHASE 9 — PRIVACY BOUNDARIES

Barkly Access is designed around privacy and user autonomy.

The creator's own analytics should be restricted to an aggregate count of how many people are using Barkly Access.

Do not implement creator-side collection of:

- Discord messages.
- Conversation contents.
- User conversation histories.
- Individual behavioral profiles.
- Disability or diagnosis information.
- Individual usage activity.
- User-specific analytics.
- Personal identifiers.
- Fingerprinting.
- Unnecessary telemetry.

Usability testing must be separately designed, voluntary and privacy-preserving.

Usability measurements intended to evaluate the interface should not become creator-side user surveillance.

